1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,120
And now, to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:20,460
So three scout leaders, two men and one woman, are attending a training event, and they're doing a hike, and they get to a river, and they need to get across, and the first man says, well, the scout is prepared.

3
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,920
So he gets out his hatchet, and he has some rope, and he's going to do a pioneering project to get him across the river.

4
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:31,420
And the second man says, a scout is physically fit, so I'm going to swim across the river.

5
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:37,000
And the woman asks directions and finds there's a bridge about 50 yards upstream.

6
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:41,620
This is podcast number 10.

7
00:00:55,920 --> 00:00:56,920
Hello.

8
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:18,160
Welcome back to the Scoutmasters podcast.

9
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:22,540
Once again, thanks to all of you who have sent emails or left comments and ratings on iTunes.

10
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:24,320
I sure do appreciate it.

11
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:31,199
This time around, we're talking about in Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less, some thoughts about the patrol system.

12
00:01:31,839 --> 00:01:44,380
Then there's an interesting story about cleaning up after camping, some thoughts about choosing a troop, and then we're going to end up with a Scoutmasters minute about, quote, difficult, unquote, scouts.

13
00:01:44,899 --> 00:01:48,000
So I hope you enjoy it, and we're going to go ahead and get started.

14
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:53,380
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

15
00:01:54,320 --> 00:02:03,020
First post in 1920.

16
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:08,359
Baden Powell put together a small booklet called Aids to Scoutmastership.

17
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:13,299
In it, you will find the basis of the scouting movement as explained by its founder.

18
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:18,780
And I think it's a particularly valuable resource for Scoutmasters and Scout leaders.

19
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:27,520
I'll have a PDF version of Aids to Scoutmastership on the blog at scoutmaster.typepad.com so that you can share this.

20
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:30,480
During this podcast, I want to talk about the patrol system.

21
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:33,580
The patrol really is the irreducible unit of scouting.

22
00:02:33,580 --> 00:02:40,320
You can have a troop, but if you don't have patrols, you're leaving out the heart of scouting.

23
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:50,260
What Baden Powell had to say about this, the patrol system is the one essential feature in which scout training differs from that of all other organizations.

24
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:55,780
And where the system is properly applied, it is absolutely bound to bring success.

25
00:02:55,780 --> 00:02:57,000
It can't help itself.

26
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:00,200
It's absolutely bound to bring success.

27
00:03:00,940 --> 00:03:09,620
The formation of boys into patrols from six to eight and training them as separate units, each under its own responsible leader, is the key to a good troop.

28
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:12,780
You can't have a good troop without having good patrols.

29
00:03:13,640 --> 00:03:15,500
So how do we have good patrols?

30
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:19,480
One of the ideas is patrols have to be independent functioning units.

31
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:21,140
They have to have their own leadership.

32
00:03:21,440 --> 00:03:25,080
Every boy has to have responsibility within the patrol.

33
00:03:25,600 --> 00:03:31,520
They need to be able to work together well to achieve whatever goals they set for themselves.

34
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,260
This is where scouting really happens.

35
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:40,320
Now, how do we do this in today's society?

36
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:41,700
How do we make this happen?

37
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:44,160
Well, there's a couple of impediments to this.

38
00:03:44,160 --> 00:03:49,840
One of them is that scouting used to be and patrols used to be much more geographically based.

39
00:03:50,140 --> 00:03:54,480
I mean, in a small town or in a city, boys lived in the same neighborhood.

40
00:03:54,680 --> 00:03:57,560
They went to the same school, and they were pals.

41
00:03:57,880 --> 00:04:04,720
And this was the natural gang that Baden-Powell talks about that would be formed into a patrol.

42
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:08,280
This is just kind of a natural affinity group.

43
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:16,000
And so having a patrol meeting would be easy because they could just walk over to another guy's house and have their patrol meeting, and that would be it.

44
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:27,120
Now, today, if your part of the country is like my part of the country, and I'll just assume that it is for the sake of argument, most of your scouts are being driven to the meeting.

45
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:38,260
Most of your scouts are riding a bus to school, and they may live 15, 20 minutes apart from their counterparts, their buddies and their friends, that kind of natural gang.

46
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:43,560
So making patrols up out of a geographic location is really not very practicable anymore.

47
00:04:44,420 --> 00:04:49,660
Now, our troop gets together and meets three or four weeks every month on a Monday night.

48
00:04:49,660 --> 00:04:55,860
And this is what we came up with to try and reemphasize the patrol aspect of scouting.

49
00:04:56,180 --> 00:05:05,680
And that is that we turned one of our troop meeting nights on a Monday, at least one every month, sometimes two, into patrol meetings.

50
00:05:06,020 --> 00:05:08,380
There would be no troop trappings.

51
00:05:08,580 --> 00:05:11,200
There would be no opening for the troop or anything like that.

52
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:16,320
It would be you get together that night with your patrol, and you work on what your patrol wants to work on.

53
00:05:16,420 --> 00:05:17,920
Well, what does a patrol want to work on?

54
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:20,640
Oh, it really depends on the scouts.

55
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:26,920
They might want to work on some kind of advancement work, or they might want to hone one particular skill.

56
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:29,920
They might want to just play a game of basketball.

57
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:32,120
They might want to take a hike.

58
00:05:32,460 --> 00:05:35,980
They might want to go up to the local pizza parlor and have pizza.

59
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:43,720
Whatever it is they're doing, they need to do it on the patrol system and with their leadership and everything like that.

60
00:05:43,720 --> 00:05:50,460
But they can function independent of any other oversight or any other leadership structure.

61
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:52,660
And they learn to function as a patrol.

62
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:56,700
Now, we've been doing this for the past several months, and it has a promising start.

63
00:05:57,180 --> 00:05:59,140
Our scouts are still getting used to the idea.

64
00:05:59,140 --> 00:06:04,159
But I think it's going to be one way to really strengthen the patrol concept.

65
00:06:04,659 --> 00:06:06,539
And then we'll take that onto an outing.

66
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:09,180
Let's take the patrol concept and let's go camping.

67
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:16,280
Each patrol naturally is planning and preparing and purchasing their own food for the weekend.

68
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:21,700
And this gives them one natural thing to kind of rally around and to work together as a team.

69
00:06:21,700 --> 00:06:37,440
The way we expand on that is to make sure that they are camping in their own setup and they're sharing their own tents and they are at a reasonable distance from other patrols so that they're not continually in each other's business and interfering.

70
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:40,960
How about the program for a weekend outing?

71
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:47,900
Well, what we're trying to emphasize now is that patrols need to assemble a program for that outing.

72
00:06:48,260 --> 00:06:52,440
Now, they sometimes will share that responsibility out amongst themselves.

73
00:06:52,960 --> 00:07:02,159
And each patrol will come up with an aspect of a particular theme or something like that that they can emphasize during the course of the outing.

74
00:07:02,260 --> 00:07:09,240
And they might do this in kind of a round-robin fashion and spend a couple hours working on each individual patrol's interest.

75
00:07:09,240 --> 00:07:11,400
Or they might just work by themselves.

76
00:07:12,180 --> 00:07:20,000
We've had a couple of instances where we've been able to get to camp on a Friday night and we'll all be fairly close together.

77
00:07:20,180 --> 00:07:23,540
And then during the course of the day Saturday, we send the patrols out on their own.

78
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:30,140
They'll go and find a place where they can backpack to and set up and set themselves out.

79
00:07:30,260 --> 00:07:31,660
We don't send adults with them.

80
00:07:32,200 --> 00:07:38,920
We will send somebody out to check in with them and see if there's anything they need and make sure things are running smoothly.

81
00:07:39,240 --> 00:07:42,820
But this gives them a great deal of independence and they just love it.

82
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:46,340
And they naturally sense that this is what scouting really is.

83
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:49,280
This is where it really happens at that patrol level.

84
00:07:49,800 --> 00:07:55,320
These are a couple of promising things that I think can be incorporated into just about any troop.

85
00:07:55,460 --> 00:08:01,400
Yes, we have our outings that are just basically going to be pretty much troop-based activity-wise.

86
00:08:01,400 --> 00:08:12,520
We might go to a camperee or some other similar activity in which, you know, basically the troop is the irreducible unit in a lot of ways.

87
00:08:12,700 --> 00:08:16,400
For the most part, we work really hard on emphasizing patrols.

88
00:08:17,479 --> 00:08:25,580
Now, I think competition is overemphasized sometimes and that it can be really a blunt instrument in scouting.

89
00:08:25,580 --> 00:08:31,000
And if you make everything a competition, well, you're kind of short-selling some of the ideals of scouting.

90
00:08:31,380 --> 00:08:38,880
But competition amongst patrols can be a very healthy thing if it's kept in the context of it being very positive.

91
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:47,819
So we do have an inter-patrol competition every once in a while, maybe about every five or six months in the troop, that the stealth leaders run.

92
00:08:47,820 --> 00:08:55,140
And the older scouts who are running their troop, in our troop it's called a venture patrol.

93
00:08:55,300 --> 00:08:57,480
This would be the senior patrol leader and his cohorts.

94
00:08:57,740 --> 00:09:01,720
They get to function as a patrol in this competition, and it makes for a lot of fun.

95
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:12,880
We also have a standard patrol award that is awarded on a monthly basis based on participation and advancement and patrol dynamics.

96
00:09:12,880 --> 00:09:18,020
Each patrol can earn that award by meeting certain benchmarks.

97
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:25,720
And then out of all the patrols that earn the award, one is declared the honor patrol for that month.

98
00:09:26,060 --> 00:09:31,040
Here's two or three ways to build patrols within the troop.

99
00:09:31,240 --> 00:09:32,640
But let's remember these things.

100
00:09:32,780 --> 00:09:36,220
Let's remember what our founding intentions were.

101
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:41,900
That patrols were the most important unit of scouting, not necessarily troops, but patrols.

102
00:09:41,900 --> 00:09:46,040
In order for patrols to work, they have to have a certain amount of independence,

103
00:09:46,620 --> 00:09:50,439
and they have to have some very high expectations of being able to function together.

104
00:09:50,900 --> 00:09:54,780
I'd be very interested to hear how you emphasize the patrol system in your troop.

105
