114
00:10:33,780 --> 00:10:42,120
Keith Monroe was the scoutmaster of Troop 2 in Santa Monica, California for the first 42 years

115
00:10:42,120 --> 00:10:43,420
of the troop's existence.

116
00:10:44,420 --> 00:10:50,900
He was also a writer, and he wrote for Scouting Magazine, very involved in scouting until his death

117
00:10:50,900 --> 00:10:53,100
in 2003 at the age of 88.

118
00:10:53,100 --> 00:11:02,760
Keith's pseudonym for some of his writing was Rice E. Cochran, and I have a book called

119
00:11:02,760 --> 00:11:06,020
Be Prepared, The Life and Illusions of a Scoutmaster.

120
00:11:06,100 --> 00:11:12,380
I've read a couple of excerpts from it on the podcast before, because some of it is just

121
00:11:12,380 --> 00:11:13,220
screamingly funny.

122
00:11:13,400 --> 00:11:18,480
As a matter of fact, I don't know where I got this from, but somebody told me that this book

123
00:11:18,480 --> 00:11:21,860
was the basis of the Clifton Webb movie, Mr. Scoutmaster.

124
00:11:21,860 --> 00:11:26,340
Here's part that I thought you would be interested in.

125
00:11:32,420 --> 00:11:35,860
The littered appearance of our campgrounds by the end of an outing always exasperated

126
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:38,860
me, and I insisted on thorough cleanups.

127
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:42,900
But at first, these took an interminable time.

128
00:11:43,740 --> 00:11:47,700
When our scouts were instructed to walk through camp and gather up all the trash in sight, they

129
00:11:47,700 --> 00:11:51,560
wandered around like the spirits of the dam, aimless and without emotion.

130
00:11:52,360 --> 00:11:56,360
Most of the orange peels and paper stayed on the ground until I personally pointed them

131
00:11:56,360 --> 00:12:01,100
out one by one and directed some scout by name to pick each one of them up.

132
00:12:01,920 --> 00:12:04,700
At last, I discovered how to get the job done rapidly.

133
00:12:05,380 --> 00:12:09,320
Ten minutes before we were ready to depart, I announced the last thing we're going to do

134
00:12:09,320 --> 00:12:13,100
before we go home is to auction off these candy bars I have here.

135
00:12:19,080 --> 00:12:21,620
The bidding will be by pieces of trash.

136
00:12:22,340 --> 00:12:25,320
Whichever scouts collect the most trash will be able to bid the highest.

137
00:12:26,120 --> 00:12:28,480
You've got eight minutes to pick up everything you can find.

138
00:12:28,480 --> 00:12:35,200
That always worked, because ten minutes later, with the camp as bare as if jackals and scavengers

139
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:39,340
had been there, and with the victorious scouts munching their candy bars, and with the scout

140
00:12:39,340 --> 00:12:45,040
master smug in the knowledge that he had mastered his scouts once again, we all went home rejoicing.

141
00:12:47,840 --> 00:12:52,220
You know what I found really funny here, because I can picture this in my mind.

142
00:12:52,220 --> 00:13:04,560
When our scouts were instructed to walk through the camp and gather up all the trash in sight,

143
00:13:04,660 --> 00:13:09,580
they wandered out about like the spirits of the damned, aimless and without a mulch.

144
