218
00:18:40,479 --> 00:18:40,979
Do, do, do.

219
00:18:40,979 --> 00:18:42,979
Do, do, do.

220
00:18:43,800 --> 00:18:45,560
Hey, scouting is not all peaches and cream.

221
00:18:45,639 --> 00:18:46,080
It's great.

222
00:18:46,280 --> 00:18:50,500
But it's not all, it's not all just sailing along on flowery beds of ease.

223
00:18:51,240 --> 00:18:53,500
Every once in a while, difficulty arises.

224
00:18:54,159 --> 00:18:56,100
And you're going to have some difficult scouts.

225
00:18:57,580 --> 00:19:01,479
And when I say difficult, it might be difficult in many different ways.

226
00:19:01,540 --> 00:19:02,379
It might be behavior.

227
00:19:02,379 --> 00:19:05,399
They might just be kind of cussed.

228
00:19:05,400 --> 00:19:14,080
It may be a physical or developmental barrier of some kind that causes you some difficulty.

229
00:19:15,080 --> 00:19:22,540
You know, I'm talking about learning disabilities or physical capabilities, lack of physical capabilities.

230
00:19:22,540 --> 00:19:30,620
You know, we really have to educate ourselves to meet the challenge of working with boys who are challenged in one way or another.

231
00:19:30,620 --> 00:19:38,300
I think we really, we really have to guard against making broad assumptions and having prejudices.

232
00:19:38,300 --> 00:19:45,580
And we have to really think and study what's going on.

233
00:19:45,580 --> 00:20:05,220
Amanda at Ballast Existence, a blog written by a lady who is, has been excluded and bullied her whole life and who has had difficulties because of her autism.

234
00:20:05,220 --> 00:20:17,360
Yet is, you know, however physically limited she may be, is just brilliantly eloquent in writing about her disability.

235
00:20:18,660 --> 00:20:25,340
And here's something that she wrote that I think is important for us as scout leaders to understand.

236
00:20:26,220 --> 00:20:27,360
She says this,

237
00:20:27,360 --> 00:20:35,620
I've read in the past about schools where children work together to solve problems and where the kids who learn faster in any particular area,

238
00:20:35,780 --> 00:20:39,260
instead of being separated out and told they're special and above everyone else,

239
00:20:39,439 --> 00:20:43,000
end up being taught to use their talents to cooperate with the other children.

240
00:20:43,840 --> 00:20:46,120
Winning or getting ahead isn't the goal here.

241
00:20:46,260 --> 00:20:54,560
They, and all the children, tend to learn more than they do in the more kind of, you know, competitive cutthroat schools.

242
00:20:54,560 --> 00:21:00,919
If schools have to exist, that sounds like a much better set of principles to run them on.

243
00:21:01,700 --> 00:21:04,580
Children aren't born knowing how to behave towards other children.

244
00:21:06,080 --> 00:21:07,120
I'll say that again.

245
00:21:07,440 --> 00:21:10,460
Children aren't born knowing how to behave towards other children.

246
00:21:11,179 --> 00:21:13,860
None of them are autistic or non-autistic.

247
00:21:14,260 --> 00:21:18,179
They have to learn that everyone's dependent on everyone else.

248
00:21:18,679 --> 00:21:23,220
That people aren't better than others just by being better at something.

249
00:21:23,220 --> 00:21:36,460
And that the tendencies to do bad things to other people are things we all have to fight and not give into if we want society to be remotely just to anyone.

250
00:21:36,460 --> 00:21:43,960
That's one nice thing about scouting is, see, we're not competitive and we're not exclusive and we're not elitist.

251
00:21:44,240 --> 00:21:45,060
We're not cutthroat.

252
00:21:45,820 --> 00:21:50,020
At its best, we're a safe haven for learning to live and work with just about anyone,

253
00:21:50,280 --> 00:21:55,540
no matter what their abilities or disabilities, and being able to benefit from the experience.

254
00:21:55,540 --> 00:22:15,000
So that's all for Scoutmaster Podcast number 10.

255
